so with that out of the way first let's start with

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.080
If there's one easy way for a new anime to grab my attention

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.779
It's just by knowing that it's an anime original series

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.759
Firstly, because this means we don't have any source material, meaning we don't get those manga-readers acting like 200 IQ

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.839
Geniuses being like "oh Is that what you think is going to happen? Poor Anime only watchers? hehehe"

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:51.240
"Look at me. I'm so smart because I know what's gonna happen next hehehe," and then light novel readers are there like

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.980
"AHAHAHA, FOOLISH MORTALS!!"

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:01.490
And secondly because it's not based on a pre-existing IP, it's likely that it will have an ending that's conclusive or even better

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:08.029
It means the entire story from beginning to end is already pre-planned and anime original series are not common occurrences

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.959
But many shows older and new stick out to me because of this fact: Cowboy Bebop

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:17.630
Eureka 7, Code Geass, even in more recent times with RE:Creators, and A Place Further than the Universe, of course

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:22.219
It wasn't just the ending that made these show stand out, but it did help a lot something like

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.840
Zankyou no Terror, despite a lot of the problems

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.729
I had with it stick with me clearly because it felt like a complete story that was planned out with an ending

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.910
I've really enjoyed and like holy shit Re:Creators did this

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:38.149
Revolutionary thing in anime called an epilogue where the entire last episode was no action, no plot.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:44.209
It was just dedicated to tying up loose ends and seeing what characters were up to after the story was concluded to leave the viewer

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:45.660
with a sense of closure

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:51.889
What The Fuc- but of course just because something is anime original doesn't mean it's guaranteed to have a good ending

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:58.850
In fact, there used to be this thing called 'Gainax endings' where certain shows coincidentally produced by studio Gainax would get endings

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:02.689
That made no sense or served sequel hooks to sequels that were never made

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.010
Fortunately 'Gainax Endings'

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:09.590
nowadays seems to have died with the decline of the studio only to find themselves Isekai-d into Trigger for its own light

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:10.970
novel adaptation of 'that time

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:12.979
I got Reincarnated as Darling in the FranXX'

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:19.010
But even some anime that have had a conclusive enough endings, have had a reoccurring issue because if it gets enough success and popularity

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:23.359
There's always a chance that the creators felt it was appropriate to revive it and have another go

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:35.310
There are little things that are sacred in anime these days and one of them certainly isn't the legacy of a popular franchise

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.570
It doesn't matter how good or how conclusive and complete your series can be when it's over

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.869
There's always gonna be a way to Ret-Conn or ass-pull or literally

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:49.340
Resurrect a new narrative to milk a few more dollars out of beloved franchises just in time to give me my 'JI-BUN WOES'

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:50.600
(cries for mistakes made that cause woes)

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:56.600
On the rare occasion you get something like Diebuster adding a new story in the same world years after Gunbuster

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:02.728
but there are numerous examples of sequels coming in that not only result in a vastly inferior ending to the original series but even going

00:05:02.729 --> 00:05:05.489
As far as completely fucking up and ruining the established canon

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:11.159
We went from Darker Than Black season 1 Chinese electric Batman to Darker Than Black season 2

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:15.419
Alcoholic dad simulator, Madoka Rebellion was a sequel movie....

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:23.009
that people had opinions about and then there's Eureka 7 AO. Now if AO is viewed as a standalone series it would be

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.499
Fine, just a fairly forgettable average show

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.550
But as a sequel

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.449
It's a fucking travesty.

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:32.000
Introducing plot elements that completely broke characters and themes from the original

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.500
going as far to confirm that the fucking spin-off reboot movie was somehow canon

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.200
in some bizarre multiverse time travel fuck ass-ury

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.500
and just in general trampling all over the world and themes of the original series

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.709
There's a discussion to be had on whether the existence of these sequels have ruined the value of the original ending

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:52.079
but there are also a few shows that have had pretty decent endings in the sense that they concluded everything they needed to

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.849
but they still left room for a sequel which were eventually made that really didn't live up to the original

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:02.099
The first Psycho Pass had some truly interesting and brilliant ideas that were explored and has yet to be replicated,

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.600
nothing from Steins;Gate either from its sequel movie or Zero

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.700
came close to capturing the original spirit and the only thing I thought the

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:12.299
New FLCL (Fooly Cooly) brought to the table was a few new great tracks from The Pillows

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:18.090
I think my problem with all these is that they are examples where sequel definitely wasn't needed or originally planned

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.130
But they made one anyway

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:26.010
It's not like the Mob Psycho season two's of today where the storyline didn't end and the sequel continues to add and enhance the plots

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.400
But the original series has said everything it wants to and put a full-stop in it,

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.380
making it hard for any sequel to capture that

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:36.020
Same magic, even if in some cases it had left space for further ideas to be explored.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.300
However, to go in the complete opposite direction,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:44.700
you sometimes get anime that choose to give itself a conclusive ending even if the original story actually continues

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.420
*RIP headphone users*

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:55.900
Kind of a lost art nowadays are anime that follow the source material up to a point only to diverge from it, giving it an ending

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:56.659
That's more conclusive

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:02.280
Either because the source material they are adapting from is still incomplete or far too long to cover in the episode counts they are given

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:04.140
now when I say some lost art

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:08.540
I mean it kind of dug its own grave, set itself from fire, and jumped in head first, Soul Eater

00:07:08.809 --> 00:07:12.849
Claymore, Hellsing, Blue Exorcist, Gantz, Akeme Ga Kill! while all of these shows had

00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:19.359
conclusive endings that stopped to the point before the manga had instead of achieving their purpose of ending the series in a satisfying way it

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.289
Just made fans want to end themselves. the problem with anime original endings

00:07:23.289 --> 00:07:26.889
Is that when an anime tries to go a different direction in order to give an ending that's more conclusive

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.579
You miss out on the proper build-up and foreshadowing to make this happen

00:07:30.909 --> 00:07:36.248
This is made even more confusing when you get sequel seasons, that continue to follow the manga, for an anime

00:07:36.248 --> 00:07:38.340
That's already been given a conclusion.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:43.020
in most cases, You can just pretend the final anime only arc didn't happen and just continue business as usual

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.940
but occasionally you get something like Tokyo Ghoul Root A

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:52.020
The completely anime only sequel to Tokyo Ghoul aka Tokyo ghoul season 2, not to be confused with

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:59.000
Tokyo Ghoul:re season 2 which is the second season to Tokyo Ghoul:re. the sequel season to Tokyo Ghoul because

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:05.860
Tokyo Ghoul Root A aka Season 2 doesn't follow the manga so that makes Tokyo Ghoul:re the cannon second season of Tokyo Ghoul

